<PageLayout
  title="About Moshakha"
  description="Who is behind Moshakha, how we research car cleaning and detailing gear, and why our recommendations are not for sale."
  canonical="/about"
>
  <p>
    Moshakha publishes buying guides, how-to guides and product reviews for car care and
    accessories — paint and interiors, wheels and tyres, and the tools, chemicals and gear
    that go with them. We started with microfibre because it is the category where marketing
    claims and reality drift furthest apart. It turned out not to be unusual.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
  <p>
    Car gear has an unusual problem: almost every category is sold on a single headline
    number that does not predict performance. Microfibre is sold on GSM, which measures
    density rather than quality. Jump starters are sold on peak amps, which no two brands
    measure the same way. Dash cams are sold on 4K, when the sensor decides whether you can
    read a number plate at night. Pressure washers are sold on PSI, when flow rate does the
    cleaning. The pattern repeats everywhere we look.
  </p>
  <p>
    We started Moshakha to write the guide we wanted when buying our first proper drying
    towel: one that explains what the number on the label actually does, then tells you what
    to buy. We now apply the same test to everything else that goes in or on a car.
  </p>

  <h2>How we research</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Specification analysis.</strong> What each published number actually measures, and whether it predicts anything you will notice in use.</li>
    <li><strong>Hands-on use</strong> where we have the product, described as hands-on use.</li>
    <li><strong>Aggregated long-term feedback</strong> where we don't — looking specifically for failure patterns after 20+ washes, which is where cheap microfibre falls apart.</li>
    <li><strong>Re-checks.</strong> Guides carry a last-updated date. Products get discontinued and reformulated; a guide that never changes is a guide nobody maintains.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We are explicit on every page about which of these applies, rather than implying testing we
    haven't done.
  </p>

  <h2>How we make money</h2>
  <p>
    Through affiliate commission, primarily Amazon Associates. No brand can pay for a place in
    our rankings and we don't run sponsored posts. The full explanation is on our
    <a href="/affiliate-disclosure">affiliate disclosure</a> page.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If we get something wrong, tell us — <a href="/contact">contact us here</a>. Corrections are
    noted on the article itself rather than quietly edited away.
  </p>
</PageLayout>
